      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.194,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "TRIANGLES"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.104,
            0.897,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "81"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.139,
            0.508,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, from (1), (2) and (3), we have :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.171,
            0.44,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": "{ \\frac { \\mathrm { A D } } { \\mathrm { D B } } } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { A E } } { \\mathrm { E C } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.222,
            0.9,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": "Is the converse of this theorem also true (For the meaning of converse, see Appendix 1)? To examine this, let us perform the following activity:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.273,
            0.547,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 3 :** Draw an angle XAY on your notebook and on ray AX, mark points $\\mathbf { B } _ { 1 } ,   \\mathbf { B } _ { 2 } ,$ $\\mathbf { B } _ { 3 } ,   \\mathbf { B } _ { 4 }$ and B such that $\\mathrm{AB}_{1}=\\mathrm{B}_{1} \\mathrm{B}_{2}=\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{B}_{3}=$ $\\mathbf { \\widetilde { B _ { 3 } } } \\mathbf { \\widetilde { B _ { 4 } } } = \\mathbf { B _ { 4 } } \\mathbf { B }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.358,
            0.547,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, on ray AY, mark points $C_{1}, C_{2}, C_{3}, C_{4}$ and C such that $\\mathrm{AC}_{1} = \\mathrm{C}_{1}\\mathrm{C}_{2}$ $C_{2}C_{3}=C_{3}C_{4}=C_{4}C$ . Then join $\\mathrm { B _ { 1 } C _ { 1 } }$ and BC (see Fig. 6.11)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.574,
            0.27,
            0.895,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.474,
            0.247,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "Note that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.457,
            0.652,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{1}}{\\mathrm{B}_{1}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{1}}{\\mathrm{C}_{1}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(\\text { Each equal } \\cup \\frac{1}{4}\\right)",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.512,
            0.826,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "You can also see that lines $\\mathrm{B_{1}C_{1}}$ and BC are parallel to each other, i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.537,
            0.896,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "B _{1} C _{1}\\parallel  BC\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.563,
            0.665,
            0.584
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, by joining $\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2}, \\mathrm{B}_{3} \\mathrm{C}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm { B _ { 4 } C _ { 4 } } ;$ you can see that:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.299,
            0.595,
            0.896,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{2}}{\\mathrm{B}_{2}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{2}}{\\mathrm{C}_{2}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(=\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\text { and }\\mathrm{B}_{2}\\mathrm{C}_{2}\\parallel\\mathrm{BC}\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.656,
            0.896,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{3}}{\\mathrm{B}_{3}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{3}}{\\mathrm{C}_{3}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(=\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\text { and }\\mathrm{B}_{3}\\mathrm{C}_{3}\\parallel\\mathrm{BC}\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.3,
            0.715,
            0.896,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{\\mathrm{AB}_{4}}{\\mathrm{B}_{4}\\mathrm{B}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{AC}_{4}}{\\mathrm{C}_{4}\\mathrm{C}}\\left(=\\frac{4}{1}\\right)\\text { and }\\mathrm{B}_{4}\\mathrm{C}_{4}\\parallel\\mathrm{BC}\\tag{4}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.773,
            0.901,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": "From (1), (2), (3) and (4), it can be observed that if a line divides two sides of a triangle in the same ratio, then the line is parallel to the third side."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.563,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.819,
            0.903,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "You can repeat this activity by drawing any angle XAY of different measure and taking any number of equal parts on arms AX and AY . Each time, you will arrive at the same result. Thus, we obtain the following theorem, which is the converse of Theorem 6.1:"
        }
      ]
    }
